   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>This lettercard is intended for your publishers’ eyes. Of course I will have the proofs read to me by <name type="person" key="leon93">Léon</name> and help you in every way I can.</p>
                    <p>I shall write on many other subjects tomorrow.</p>
                    <p>The use of my name in advts would be harmful to you and to me. Let the book stand on its own sea legs. I should not appear as handing flowers to myself nor you as writing at my dictation. I have 20 years experience of English publishers and know the mongrels better than you do.</p>
                    <p>I did not go to <name type="geo" key="G_CHE_ZURI">Z’ch</name> (this abbreviation you c’d p’r’ps slip in too) nor come here on a/c of my eyes and <name type="person" key="vogt79">Vogt</name> but on a/c of <name type="person" key="joyc07">Lucia</name>. I have had 3 dreadful months of worry &amp; expense but I think the worst is definitely over. She is here with us. Keep all this to yourself please. I do not grudge the expense as I mean to do all I can to get the poor child right – &amp; will.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">J.J.</name>
                        </signed>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="global-note1" type="global">JJ date and postmark match. Inserted ALI is undated, but context matches the main letter.</note>
                <note xml:id="global-note2" type="global">The ALS dated 21 August 1933 was published in <name type="work">Letters III</name>. The ALI addendum is unpublished, but its context identifies it as accompanying the main letter</note>
            </div>
